  "title": {
    "template": "title",
    "heading": "The multi-store model of memory",
    "lead": "Recall: what do encoding, storage and retrieval mean?",
    "picture": null
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Three stores, three sets of properties",
      "lead": "Compare the stores. Which holds the least information?",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "Typical coding, capacity and duration of the stores in the multi-store model",
        "alt": "The sensory register has very brief duration; STM has limited capacity and duration; LTM has potentially unlimited capacity and duration.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["Store", "Coding", "Capacity", "Duration"],
        "rows": [
          ["Sensory register", "Sense-specific", "Very large", "Very brief"],
          ["STM", "Mainly acoustic", "About 7 ± 2 items", "About 18–30 seconds"],
          ["LTM", "Mainly semantic", "Potentially unlimited", "Potentially lifelong"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "discussion",
      "heading": "Predict what stays",
      "lead": "A student reads a message while an announcement plays. What happens to the announcement if they pay it no attention?",
      "picture": {
        "shows": "a student looking closely at a phone while a nearby loudspeaker plays an announcement",
        "must_see": ["student watching phone", "nearby wall loudspeaker", "open classroom door"],
        "subject": "generic"
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Trace information through the model",
      "lead": "Follow the arrows. Explain the role of each process.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The multi-store model with attention, maintenance rehearsal, transfer to LTM and retrieval",
        "alt": "Attention transfers sensory information to STM. Maintenance rehearsal keeps it there and may transfer it to LTM. Retrieval returns information to STM.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Sensory register", "Short-term memory", "Long-term memory"],
        "links": [
          {
            "from": 0,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Attention"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Maintenance rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 2,
            "label": "Prolonged rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 2,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Retrieval"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Worked example: remembering a code",
      "lead": "Leah hears 6–2–9, repeats it aloud and recalls it tomorrow.",
      "points": [
        "Attention selects the sounds for STM.",
        "Repetition maintains the code in STM.",
        "The model proposes rehearsal transfers it to LTM."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The stores involved in hearing, repeating and later recalling a code",
        "alt": "Leah attends to a code, rehearses it in STM, stores it in LTM and retrieves it into STM the next day.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Sensory register", "STM: acoustic code", "LTM: stored code"],
        "links": [
          {
            "from": 0,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Attention"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 2,
            "label": "Rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 2,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Retrieval tomorrow"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Your turn: explain the route",
      "questions": [
        "Amir hears 4–8–1, repeats it aloud and recalls it tomorrow. Trace its route through the stores and name each transfer process.",
        "What coding is most likely while Amir repeats the code aloud? Explain your answer.",
        "Amir instead hears a new code once, then counts backwards for 30 seconds. Predict his recall and explain why."
      ],
      "instruction": "Answer using the multi-store model.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Glanzer and Cunitz: serial position",
      "lead": "After a 30-second filled delay, the early-item advantage remained but the late-item advantage largely disappeared.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "Schematic serial position curves, not original numerical data, comparing immediate recall with recall after a filled delay",
        "alt": "Immediate recall is higher for early and late words. After a filled delay, recall of early words remains high but the late-word advantage largely disappears.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {
          "label": "Position in word list",
          "min": 1,
          "max": 10,
          "step": 1
        },
        "y": {
          "label": "Recall",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 1,
          "step": null
        },
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": true,
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "Immediate",
            "points": [[1, 0.9], [2, 0.7], [3, 0.5], [4, 0.35], [5, 0.3], [6, 0.3], [7, 0.35], [8, 0.5], [9, 0.7], [10, 0.9]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          },
          {
            "label": "Filled delay",
            "points": [[1, 0.88], [2, 0.68], [3, 0.48], [4, 0.33], [5, 0.28], [6, 0.28], [7, 0.28], [8, 0.28], [9, 0.3], [10, 0.3]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          }
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": [
          {
            "x": 1,
            "y": 0.98,
            "label": "Primacy"
          },
          {
            "x": 10,
            "y": 0.98,
            "label": "Recency"
          }
        ],
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "steps",
      "heading": "Worked evaluation: the recency effect",
      "points": [
        "Evidence: the late-item advantage largely disappeared after a filled delay.",
        "Explanation: counting prevented rehearsal, so recent items were no longer available in STM.",
        "Judgement: this supports a brief STM store distinct from LTM, but does not prove every part of the model."
      ],
      "figure": null
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Your turn: build an evaluation",
      "questions": [
        "Glanzer and Cunitz found that the early-item advantage remained after a 30-second filled delay. State this finding precisely.",
        "Use rehearsal and the properties of the stores to explain this finding.",
        "Explain how the finding supports the model. Add one reason it does not prove the whole model."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write a short evidence → explanation → judgement paragraph.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Is STM really one store?",
      "lead": "Shallice and Warrington studied KF after brain injury.",
      "points": [
        {
          "label": "Challenge",
          "text": "Uneven impairment suggests STM has separate components."
        },
        {
          "label": "Caution",
          "text": "One unusual case limits generalisation."
        }
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "KF’s contrasting short-term memory performance following brain injury",
        "alt": "KF had severely impaired verbal short-term memory, particularly for heard material, but relatively preserved visual short-term memory.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["STM task", "KF"],
        "rows": [
          ["Verbal", "Impaired"],
          ["Visual", "Spared"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Apply the model—and test its limits",
      "questions": [
        "A museum guide gives Nia six spoken directions. She repeats them silently. Name the likely store and coding, and explain what repetition does.",
        "After a 30-second distraction, Nia forgets the final directions but remembers the first ones. Explain this pattern using the model.",
        "Stretch: a patient recalls pictures well but struggles with spoken digits. Explain why this challenges the model, and qualify your conclusion."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write explanations that connect each detail to the model.",
      "picture": null
    }
  ]
}
